e. empower women and children victims of acts of violence to be more empowered physically, psychologically, socially, and economically.

Article 8

(1) Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 7, is implemented by Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in the fields of:

a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population administration and civil registration:
f. women's empowerment, protection of women and children; g. cooperatives and Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises; and
h. public tranquility, public order, and community protection
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 7

(1) Regional Government conducts prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter a through empowerment and awareness raising to Families, Parents, and the Community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness raising as referred to in paragraph (1), it is implemented by means of:

a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in both formal and informal education;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. establishing and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. establishing networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, educational institutions, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in every sub-district.

Article 2

Protection of women and children from acts of violence is organized based on the principles of:

a. humanity;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. order and legal certainty;
e. transparency; and
f. protection.

Article 17

Forms of violence against women and children include:

a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. maltreatment;
f. exploitation; and/or
g. other violence.

Article 18

Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights: a. the right to be respected for their dignity and worth as human beings; b. the right to recovery;

c. the right to determine their own decisions;

d. the right to obtain information;

e. the right to confidentiality;

f. the right to social rehabilitation;

g. the right to handling of complaints in a quick, precise, comfortable manner and according to needs;

h. the right of victims and their families to receive facilitation in the judicial process;

i. the right to assistance; and

j. the right to a sense of security and protection.

Article 14

(1) Obligations and responsibilities of Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a, cover:

a. establishing, implementing policies, programs, and conducting cooperation of activities in the administration of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of women and children from acts of violence as well as providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating budget for the administration of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the administration of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protective measure for children who become victims of acts of violence.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

