d. the right to obtain information;

e. the right to confidentiality;

f. the right to social rehabilitation;

g. the right to handling of complaints in a quick, precise, comfortable manner and according to needs;

h. the right of victims and their families to receive facilitation in the judicial process;

i. the right to assistance; and

j. the right to a sense of security and protection.

Article 10

Regional Government conducts recovery as referred to in Article 6 letter c, covering:

a. providing physical recovery at health service institutions; b. providing medico-legal services;
c. assisting in the repatriation of victims;
d. providing temporary protection in a Safe House; e. providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;
f. providing spiritual guidance services; and/or
g. conducting preparation of family, school, work and community environments, as well as economic empowerment.

Article 19

Child victims of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 18, also receive special rights as follows:

a. right to survival, growth and development;
b. right to basic population services;
c. right to equal protection;
d. right to be free from various stigma; and
e. right to obtain freedom.

Article 9

Regional Government provides legal protection as referred to in Article 6 letter b, covering:

a. providing protection in a Safe House;
b. providing legal information to victims;
c. conducting assistance for victims as witnesses from the investigation process to the verdict; and
d. providing special legal protection for child victims of acts of violence that may be carried out by appointing guardianship in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of protection of women and children from acts of violence includes:

a. implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence; b. obligations and responsibilities;
c. forms of violence;
d. rights of women and children from acts of violence;
e. institutions;
f. cooperation and partnership;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. awards; and
i. funding;

Article 3

The objectives of protection of women and children from acts of violence are to:

a. prevent acts of violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of acts of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect, provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children from acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. empower women and children victims of acts of violence to be more empowered physically, psychologically, socially, and economically.

Article 14

(1) Obligations and responsibilities of Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a, cover:

a. establishing, implementing policies, programs, and conducting cooperation of activities in the administration of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of women and children from acts of violence as well as providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating budget for the administration of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the administration of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protective measure for children who become victims of acts of violence.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 6

