d. building community participation and concern for the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. establishing and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. establishing networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, educational institutions, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in every sub-district.

Article 8

(1) Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 7, is implemented by Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in the fields of:

a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population administration and civil registration:
f. women's empowerment, protection of women and children; g. cooperatives and Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises; and
h. public tranquility, public order, and community protection
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 23

(1) The Regional Government develops cooperation patterns for the protection of women and children from violence.

(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is in order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out between the Regional Government and:

a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regional Governments;
d. Higher Education Institutions;
e. Social and Religious Institutions;
f. Non-Governmental Organizations; and/or
g. Media.
(4) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses.
(5) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 29

(1) Every person who does not fulfill their obligations as referred to in Article 15 paragraph (2) and Article 16 shall be subject to administrative sanctions.

(2) Administrative sanctions to persons as referred to in paragraph (1) are in the form of:

a. oral warning;
b. written warning; and/or
c. administrative fine
(3) Administrative sanctions to persons as referred to in paragraph (1) are in the form of:
a. written warning;
b. temporary cessation of activities;
c. permanent cessation of activities;
d. temporary revocation of license;
e. permanent revocation of license; and/or
f. administrative fine.
(4) Administrative fines as referred to in paragraph (2) letter c and paragraph (3) letter f constitute Regional revenue.
(5) Further provisions regarding the procedures for imposing administrative sanctions are regulated in Regent Regulation.

Article 10

Regional Government conducts recovery as referred to in Article 6 letter c, covering:

a. providing physical recovery at health service institutions; b. providing medico-legal services;
c. assisting in the repatriation of victims;
d. providing temporary protection in a Safe House; e. providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;
f. providing spiritual guidance services; and/or
g. conducting preparation of family, school, work and community environments, as well as economic empowerment.

